exec(open(__file__.rsplit("/",1)[0] + "/_h.py").read())
B("Isaiah", """
1|God says Judah has rebelled like children who forgot their father. He wants justice, not empty sacrifices. Though your sins are like scarlet, they shall be white as snow.
2|In the last days all nations will go up to the Lord's mountain. They will beat their swords into plowshares. The proud will be brought low.
3|God removes Judah's leaders and supports. The women of Jerusalem, proud and vain, will be humbled.
4|After the judgment, those who are left in Zion will be called holy, and God's glory will cover the city like a canopy.
5|The song of the vineyard: God planted a vineyard and it gave wild grapes. Woe to those who call evil good and good evil.
6|Isaiah sees the Lord on his throne, and the seraphim cry, Holy, holy, holy. A coal touches his lips and his sin is forgiven. He answers: Here I am, send me.
7|King Ahaz is afraid of two kings. Isaiah says: the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and call his name Immanuel.
8|Isaiah has a son named Maher-shalal-hash-baz, a sign that Assyria will take Damascus and Samaria. God is a sanctuary to those who trust him.
9|The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light. A child is born to us, whose names are Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace.
10|Woe to unjust rulers. God uses Assyria as a rod, then punishes Assyria for its pride. A remnant of Israel will return.
11|A shoot will come from the stump of Jesse, and the Spirit of the Lord will rest on him. The wolf will live with the lamb.
12|A short song of thanks: God is my salvation. With joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation.
13|The oracle against Babylon: the day of the Lord is coming, and Babylon will fall like Sodom.
14|Israel will be restored. A taunt against the king of Babylon, who said he would rise to the heavens, but is brought down to the pit.
15|A message about Moab: the land is devastated and its people weep.
16|Moab asks Judah for shelter. The prophet says the land will be ruined, and Moab's pride will not save it.
17|The message about Damascus and Ephraim: they will fall. The nations roar, but God rebukes them and they flee.
18|A message about Ethiopia, a land beyond the rivers. God watches quietly, and then gifts will be brought to Mount Zion.
19|A message about Egypt: it will be afraid and divided. In that day Egypt and Assyria will worship the Lord together with Israel.
20|Isaiah walks around naked and barefoot for three years as a sign that Egypt and Ethiopia will be taken captive by Assyria.
21|Messages about Babylon, Edom and Arabia. The watchman cries: Babylon has fallen.
22|A message about Jerusalem. The people feast while the enemy is at the gates. Shebna is removed and Eliakim is given the key of David.
23|A message about Tyre, the great trading city, which will be forgotten for seventy years, then return to trade.
24|The whole earth will be laid waste because the people broke the everlasting covenant. The Lord will reign on Mount Zion.
25|A song of praise: the Lord has been a refuge for the poor. He will wipe away tears from all faces and swallow up death forever.
26|A song for Judah: you keep in perfect peace the mind that is fixed on you. The dead will live, and their bodies will rise.
27|God will punish Leviathan. In that day Israel will bloom and fill the world with fruit.
28|Woe to the drunken leaders of Ephraim. God lays in Zion a precious cornerstone, and whoever believes will not be in haste.
29|Woe to Ariel (Jerusalem). The people honor God with their lips but their hearts are far from him. The deaf will hear and the blind will see.
30|Woe to those who go to Egypt for help. In returning and rest you shall be saved. In quietness and trust shall be your strength.
31|Woe to those who rely on Egypt's horses. The Egyptians are men and not God, and the Lord will defend Jerusalem.
32|A king will reign in righteousness. The women of Jerusalem are warned that trouble is coming, until the Spirit is poured out from on high.
33|Woe to the destroyer. The Lord is exalted, and he fills Zion with justice. Your eyes will see the king in his beauty.
34|God's judgment falls on all nations, especially Edom, which becomes a desert.
35|The desert will bloom. The eyes of the blind will be opened and the lame will leap like a deer. The ransomed of the Lord will return to Zion with singing.
36|Assyria's king Sennacherib attacks Judah, and his commander mocks Hezekiah and tells the people not to trust the Lord.
37|Hezekiah takes the letter to the temple and prays. Isaiah says God will defend Jerusalem, and an angel strikes down the Assyrian camp.
38|Hezekiah is sick and prays. God hears him and adds fifteen years to his life. Hezekiah writes a song of thanks.
39|Envoys from Babylon visit, and Hezekiah shows them all his treasures. Isaiah says that everything will one day be carried off to Babylon.
40|Comfort, comfort my people. A voice cries: prepare the way of the Lord in the wilderness. Those who wait on the Lord will renew their strength.
41|God calls Israel his servant, whom he has chosen. Do not fear, for I am with you. Idols are nothing.
42|The first Servant Song: here is my servant, whom I uphold, the one who brings justice to the nations. He will not break a bruised reed. God alone is the Lord.
43|Do not fear, for I have redeemed you; I have called you by name. God says he is doing a new thing, and he blots out sins for his own sake.
44|God pours his Spirit on Israel's children. The idol maker burns half his wood for fuel and bows down to the rest. Cyrus is named as God's shepherd.
45|God calls Cyrus, a foreign king, his anointed. There is no other God. Every knee will bow to him.
46|The idols of Babylon are carried on animals, but God carries his people from birth to old age.
47|Babylon, once proud, is told to sit in the dust. Her magic and astrologers cannot save her.
48|God has always declared what will happen so that Israel could not credit idols. They are told to leave Babylon.
49|The second Servant Song: the Lord called me from the womb. He will be a light to the nations. Can a woman forget her nursing child? I will not forget you.
50|The third Servant Song: the Servant gives his back to those who strike him and does not hide his face from shame. The Lord helps him.
51|Listen to me, you who pursue righteousness. Look to Abraham your father. The ransomed of the Lord will return with singing.
52|Awake, Zion. How beautiful on the mountains are the feet of the one who brings good news. The fourth Servant Song begins: my servant shall prosper.
53|The fourth Servant Song: he was pierced for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities. By his wounds we are healed. The Lord laid on him the iniquity of us all.
54|Sing, barren woman. God's covenant of peace will not be removed. No weapon formed against you shall prosper.
55|Come, all who are thirsty, come to the waters. Seek the Lord while he may be found. His word will not return to him empty.
56|Foreigners and eunuchs who hold to the covenant are welcome. My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples.
57|The righteous perish and no one notices. God dwells with the humble and brings peace to the contrite.
58|True fasting means freeing the oppressed, sharing bread with the hungry and housing the poor. Then your light will break forth like the dawn.
59|Your sins have separated you from God. He sees there is no justice and puts on righteousness like a breastplate. A Redeemer will come to Zion.
60|Arise, shine, for your light has come. Nations will come to your light and kings to your dawn. The wealth of the nations comes to you.
61|The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, to bring good news to the poor and to proclaim freedom for the captives. God gives a garland instead of ashes.
62|For Zion's sake I will not keep silent. You will be called by a new name. God rejoices over you as a bridegroom over his bride.
63|The Lord comes from Edom in blood-stained garments, having trodden the winepress. The people remember God's kindness and pray: you are our Father.
64|Oh that you would tear the heavens and come down. We are the clay and you are our potter.
65|God says he was ready to be found by those who did not seek him. He promises new heavens and a new earth, where the wolf and lamb feed together.
66|God says: heaven is my throne and the earth is my footstool. As a mother comforts her child, so I will comfort you. All nations will come to worship before him.
""")
B("Jeremiah", """
1|God calls Jeremiah before he was born to be a prophet to the nations. He is only a boy, but God puts words in his mouth and says: do not be afraid, for I am with you.
2|God says Israel was devoted to him in the wilderness, but has left him, the fountain of living water, for broken cisterns.
3|Israel is like a faithless wife. God calls her to return and promises to give them shepherds after his own heart.
4|If you return, O Israel, return to me. An enemy is coming from the north, and Jeremiah mourns for his people.
5|God asks if he can find one honest person in Jerusalem. The people are stubborn and rebellious, and will be punished.
6|Jerusalem will be besieged because of its sin. The people say: Peace, peace, when there is no peace. They refuse to walk in the ancient paths.
7|The temple sermon: do not trust in the words, this is the temple of the Lord. Change your ways. God does not want sacrifice without obedience.
8|The people's bones will be dug up and spread out. There is no balm in Gilead. They are slow to repent. The harvest is past, and we are not saved.
9|Jeremiah weeps for his people. Let no one boast in wisdom, strength or riches, but in knowing the Lord.
10|The idols of the nations are like scarecrows in a cucumber field. The Lord is the true God, the maker of the earth.
11|Judah has broken the covenant. The people of Anathoth plot to kill Jeremiah, and God promises to punish them.
12|Jeremiah asks why the wicked prosper. God tells him it will get harder, but he will uproot and then restore nations.
13|A linen belt ruined in the ground is a sign of Judah's ruin. Can the Ethiopian change his skin or the leopard its spots? Neither can you do good.
14|A drought. The people cry to God, but God says even if Moses and Samuel stood before him he would not relent.
15|God says he is tired of relenting. Jeremiah complains about his pain and God tells him to turn back and he will restore him.
16|Jeremiah is told not to marry or have children and not to attend funerals or feasts, as signs of the coming disaster. God promises a return.
17|Judah's sin is written with an iron pen. Cursed is the one who trusts in man. Blessed is the one who trusts in the Lord. Keep the Sabbath.
18|The potter reshapes the clay. God can rebuild or tear down any nation. The people plot against Jeremiah, who prays for justice.
19|Jeremiah breaks a clay jar in the Valley of Ben Hinnom to show that God will break the city.
20|The priest Pashhur beats Jeremiah and puts him in stocks. Jeremiah says God's word is a fire in his bones, and he cannot hold it in. He curses the day he was born.
21|King Zedekiah asks Jeremiah to ask God for a miracle. Jeremiah says God will fight against Jerusalem. The choice is life or death.
22|Jeremiah speaks against the kings of Judah, who ignore justice. Shallum will not return. Jehoiakim will be buried like a donkey.
23|Woe to the shepherds who scatter the flock. God will raise up a righteous Branch from David, who will reign as king. Jeremiah condemns false prophets.
24|Two baskets of figs: the good figs are the exiles in Babylon, whom God will bring back. The bad figs are Zedekiah and those who stay.
25|The people have refused to listen for twenty-three years. They will serve Babylon for seventy years. The cup of wrath is given to the nations.
26|Jeremiah preaches in the temple and is nearly killed. Some elders defend him by remembering Micah. Another prophet, Uriah, is killed.
27|Jeremiah wears a yoke to show that Judah and the nations should serve Babylon. False prophets say the opposite.
28|Hananiah says the exile will end in two years and breaks Jeremiah's yoke. Jeremiah says God will replace it with an iron one, and Hananiah dies.
29|Jeremiah's letter to the exiles: build houses, plant gardens, seek the peace of the city. After seventy years God will bring you back. I know the plans I have for you.
30|God promises to restore Israel and Judah. I will bring back my people and heal their wounds.
31|A new covenant: I will put my law within them and write it on their hearts. Rachel weeps for her children. God has loved you with an everlasting love.
32|Jeremiah buys a field while Jerusalem is under siege, as a sign that people will again buy fields in the land. Nothing is too hard for the Lord.
33|God promises to heal and restore Jerusalem. A righteous Branch will spring up from David's line, and the priests and David's house will never end.
34|Zedekiah is told he will be taken to Babylon. The people free their slaves and then take them back, breaking the covenant.
35|The Rechabites keep their ancestor's command not to drink wine. God uses them as an example for Judah's disobedience.
36|Jeremiah dictates his words to Baruch. King Jehoiakim cuts up the scroll and burns it piece by piece. God tells Jeremiah to write it again.
37|Jerusalem's siege is lifted briefly. Jeremiah is arrested as a traitor and put in prison. Zedekiah secretly asks him if there is any word from the Lord.
38|The officials throw Jeremiah in a muddy cistern. Ebed-melech, an Ethiopian, rescues him. Zedekiah is told to surrender to save his life.
39|Jerusalem falls. Zedekiah's sons are killed in front of him and he is blinded. Jeremiah is freed, and Ebed-melech is promised safety.
40|Gedaliah is made governor, and Jeremiah stays with him. Many of the scattered Jews return to the land.
41|Ishmael murders Gedaliah and others. Johanan chases Ishmael and the people are afraid of Babylon.
42|The people ask Jeremiah to pray for them, and promise to obey. Jeremiah says God wants them to stay in Judah and not go to Egypt.
43|The people refuse and go to Egypt, taking Jeremiah and Baruch with them. Jeremiah says Nebuchadnezzar will conquer Egypt.
44|Jeremiah warns the Jews in Egypt about worshipping the Queen of Heaven. They refuse, and say things were better when they did.
45|God's message to Baruch: do not seek great things for yourself. I will give you your life as a prize of war wherever you go.
46|A message against Egypt, which is defeated at Carchemish, and a promise that Israel will be saved.
47|A message about the Philistines, who will be struck down from the north.
48|A message about Moab, which was proud and trusted in its wealth. It will be destroyed, but God promises to restore it in the end.
49|Messages against Ammon, Edom, Damascus, Kedar, Hazor and Elam. God promises to restore Ammon and Elam.
50|A message about Babylon. It will fall, and Israel will be freed to return to Zion, seeking the Lord.
51|More about Babylon's fall. God is the maker of everything, and Babylon will sink and rise no more.
52|The fall of Jerusalem, told as history. The temple is burned and the people are taken to Babylon. The book ends with Jehoiachin being released from prison.
""")
B("Lamentations", """
1|Jerusalem sits alone, like a widow. She weeps in the night. She has no comforter and her friends have turned into enemies.
2|The Lord has become like an enemy. He has destroyed the city without pity. The people cry out in the streets and the prophets' visions were empty.
3|The poet speaks of his own suffering. But this I call to mind: the steadfast love of the Lord never ceases, his mercies are new every morning. Great is your faithfulness.
4|Zion's children once precious as gold now lie in the streets. Famine was cruel. The sins of the prophets and priests caused the fall.
5|A prayer for the people: remember, Lord, what has happened to us. Restore us to yourself, and we shall be restored.
""")
B("Baruch", """
1|Baruch reads the book to the exiles in Babylon. They collect money and send it to Jerusalem, with a request to pray for King Nebuchadnezzar and for themselves.
2|The exiles confess that God was right to punish them. They ask him to hear their prayer and remember the covenant.
3|A prayer for mercy. Why are we in a foreign land? We left the fountain of wisdom. Where can wisdom be found? God alone knows her.
4|Wisdom is the book of the commandments of God. Jerusalem encourages her children to be brave. God will bring them back with joy.
5|Jerusalem is told to take off her robe of mourning and put on the splendor of God's glory. God leads Israel back with joy.
6|The Letter of Jeremiah: the idols in Babylon are made by craftsmen and cannot see, hear, speak or save. They are no gods.
""")
B("Ezekiel", """
1|Ezekiel, a priest in exile in Babylon, sees a great vision of the glory of God: four living creatures, wheels within wheels, and a throne with a figure like a man.
2|God calls him son of man and sends him to a rebellious people. He is told not to be afraid, and eats a scroll.
3|Ezekiel eats the scroll, and it tastes like honey. God makes him a watchman for Israel. If he warns people, he has done his job.
4|Ezekiel acts out the siege of Jerusalem with a brick, lying on his side for many days, and eating rationed bread.
5|He shaves his head and divides the hair in three, showing how Jerusalem will be punished by sword, fire and scattering.
6|God speaks against the mountains of Israel, where people worship idols. A remnant will escape and will know that he is the Lord.
7|The end has come on the land. Silver and gold cannot save the people on the day of God's wrath.
8|Ezekiel is carried in a vision to the temple in Jerusalem, where he sees idols and the elders worshipping them in secret.
9|Six men come with weapons to strike the city. A man in linen marks the foreheads of those who grieve over the sin, and they are spared.
10|The glory of the Lord leaves the temple, as the cherubim lift the wheels.
11|The leaders of Jerusalem say the city is a cooking pot. God says they will be judged. He promises to give the exiles a new heart and a new spirit.
12|Ezekiel acts out going into exile. The people say the visions are far off, but God says none of his words will be delayed.
13|Woe to false prophets who say Peace when there is no peace. They are like men who whitewash a flimsy wall.
14|Some elders come to ask God a question, but have idols in their hearts. Even Noah, Daniel and Job could save only themselves.
15|Jerusalem is like a vine's wood, which is good for nothing but fuel.
16|God describes Jerusalem as a foundling girl he cared for and married, but who became unfaithful. He promises an everlasting covenant at the end.
17|A parable of two eagles and a vine, about Zedekiah's rebellion against Babylon. God will plant a tender shoot on a high mountain.
18|Each person is responsible for his own sin. The soul that sins is the one that will die. God takes no pleasure in the death of the wicked, but wants them to turn and live.
19|A lament for the princes of Israel, as lions caught in a pit, and a vine uprooted.
20|God recounts Israel's rebellion since Egypt. He will bring the people back from the nations and purify them.
21|The sword of the Lord is drawn against Jerusalem. The king of Babylon uses divination at a crossroads to choose his target.
22|A list of Jerusalem's sins: bloodshed, idolatry, oppression, incest and greed. God looked for someone to stand in the gap and found no one.
23|The story of two sisters, Oholah (Samaria) and Oholibah (Jerusalem), who were unfaithful to God and ended in ruin.
24|The cooking pot boils on the day Babylon attacks Jerusalem. Ezekiel's wife dies and he is told not to mourn, as a sign for the people.
25|Judgment on Ammon, Moab, Edom and Philistia, who rejoiced when Judah fell.
26|Judgment on Tyre, which rejoiced at Jerusalem's fall. Nebuchadnezzar will besiege it and the city will become a bare rock.
27|A lament for Tyre as a great merchant ship, now wrecked in the heart of the sea.
28|God speaks against the prince of Tyre for his pride. The passage describes a being in Eden whose heart was proud. God also promises Israel will dwell securely.
29|Judgment on Pharaoh and Egypt, who has been a staff of reed to Israel. Egypt will be a lowly kingdom for forty years.
30|The day of the Lord is near for Egypt. Its allies will fall by the sword.
31|Pharaoh is compared to a great cedar of Lebanon, which was cut down and sent to the pit.
32|A lament for Pharaoh. He is like a sea monster caught in a net, and sent down to Sheol with the uncircumcised.
33|Ezekiel is a watchman again. When news comes that Jerusalem has fallen, the exiles listen to him but do not act on his words.
34|God condemns the shepherds of Israel who feed themselves. He will search for his sheep and set one shepherd, his servant David, over them.
35|A judgment on Edom, who held an old hatred against Israel and wanted to take its land.
36|God promises to cleanse Israel: I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit within you. The land will be like the Garden of Eden.
37|The valley of dry bones: God breathes on the bones and they come to life as a vast army. Two sticks are joined as one: Israel and Judah will be one nation under David.
38|Gog of Magog attacks Israel with many nations. This is a vision of a great final battle, in which God shows his holiness.
39|God defeats Gog. The weapons are burned for seven years, and the dead are buried. God promises to restore Israel and pour out his Spirit.
40|In the twenty-fifth year of exile, Ezekiel is taken in a vision to a high mountain, and a man with a measuring rod shows him a new temple.
41|The inner sanctuary and the Holy of Holies are measured, with carved cherubim and palm trees on the walls.
42|The priests' chambers, where they eat the holy offerings and change clothes.
43|The glory of the Lord returns to the temple from the east. God says: this is the place of my throne, where I will dwell among the people forever. The altar is described.
44|The east gate stays shut because the Lord entered through it. The Levites who went astray are limited, and the descendants of Zadok serve as priests.
45|The land is divided for the sanctuary, the city and the prince. Just weights and the festivals are commanded.
46|Rules for the prince's offerings on the Sabbath, new moon and festivals, and for how the prince shares his land. The kitchens for boiling sacrifices.
47|A river flows from the threshold of the temple and grows deeper. It makes the salt sea fresh, and trees on its banks bear fruit each month. The boundaries of the land are given.
48|The land is divided among the twelve tribes. The city has twelve gates named for the tribes. Its name will be: The Lord is there.
""")
B("Daniel", """
1|Daniel and three friends, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah, are taken to Babylon. They refuse the royal food and are healthier on vegetables and water. God gives them wisdom.
2|Nebuchadnezzar dreams of a statue made of gold, silver, bronze, iron and clay. Daniel interprets it as a series of kingdoms, followed by God's kingdom that will never end.
3|Nebuchadnezzar sets up a gold statue. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refuse to worship it and are thrown into a fiery furnace. A fourth figure walks with them, and they are not burned. This chapter includes the Prayer of Azariah and the Song of the Three Young Men.
4|Nebuchadnezzar's dream of a great tree being cut down. Daniel says it is the king, who will live like a wild animal until he acknowledges the Most High. It happens, and then he praises God.
5|King Belshazzar holds a feast using the temple vessels. A hand writes on the wall: Mene, Mene, Tekel, Parsin. Daniel says the kingdom is ending. Belshazzar is killed that night.
6|Darius makes a law that no one may pray to any god except the king. Daniel keeps praying and is thrown into the lions' den. God shuts the lions' mouths.
7|Daniel's dream of four beasts and of one like a son of man who comes with the clouds and is given an everlasting kingdom.
8|Daniel's vision of a ram and a goat. The angel Gabriel explains that it is about future kings and a ruler who will stand against the Prince of princes.
9|Daniel prays and confesses the sins of his people. Gabriel gives him the prophecy of seventy weeks about the future of Jerusalem and an anointed one.
10|Daniel mourns for three weeks. An angelic figure appears and tells him that he has been delayed by spiritual conflict with the prince of Persia. Michael helps.
11|A long vision about the kings of the north and south in conflict, leading up to a ruler who exalts himself.
12|Michael will arise. Many who sleep in the dust will awake, some to everlasting life and some to shame. Those who lead many to righteousness will shine like the stars.
13|Susanna, a faithful wife, is falsely accused by two elders. Daniel proves her innocence by questioning the elders separately.
14|Daniel shows the king that the idol Bel does not eat the food, by scattering ash on the floor. He then kills a dragon the people worship. He is thrown into the lions' den again and is fed by the prophet Habakkuk.
""")
